The assessment is written to session_plan/assessment.md (about 11 KB) but not committed. session_plan/ is gitignored, so the planned git add was refused. I force-added it at first, then undid that local commit, which was never pushed, so the ignored folder stays out of main and the file is still on disk for the planner. The tree is clean at d05aad87.

State: the binary at HEAD runs, and a simple -p prompt returned pong. The last 5 evolve runs and the last 5 CI runs all passed, and the last 10 sessions each landed 2 of 2 tasks with no reverts.

New findings, each checked against the code:
1. A bash timeout throws away the output collected so far. StreamingBashTool (src/tools.rs ~585) collects output line by line but returns only Command timed out after Ns. So the model can't see where a hung build got stuck. My own dependency already handles this: yoagent 0.25.15 returns Output so far:. Claude Code shipped the same fix on Oct 26. The existing test only checks for the words "timed out", so nothing pins the output. This is the strongest gap I found.
2. `yoyo ast` exits 0 when it couldn't run. ast_shell_status (dispatch_sub.rs:310) only returns nonzero for a parse error, so "sg not installed" and a search error both exit 0. I reproduced the first; the search-error path couldn't be probed because sg isn't installed here.
3. The trajectory's "claimed success, 0 task commits" warning about the Day 239 08:58 session is a false alarm. extract_trajectory.py sets the clone's boundary to the earliest author time in the history. One rebased commit (68e9b580, authored Oct 24 but committed Oct 26) drags that boundary about 11.5 hours earlier than where the clone's history actually stops, so a session whose commits fall outside the clone gets accused. The fix is to take the boundary from where the history is cut, not from the earliest author time.

Already filed, still reproducing: #1087 (yoyo grep on a single file outside git reports no matches), #1088 (yoyo watch <cmd> promises something that can't happen and exits 0), #1086 (shell verbs print /verb syntax). The #1054 test still carries the disproved "own block" name.

Needs a person, not code: community issue #1089 asks for outreach on X and contributions to other projects. That would put public writes under yoyo's identity, so it should go to @yuanhao as a bounded question.

Research: I mapped Claude Code's Oct 26 release and Codex 0.163–0.164 against my open issues and saved the mapping to yopedia. Claude Code's release confirms #1057 is real, and its other fixes match #1088 and #1070. Codex's credential masking bears on #1064.
